## Why This Is Asked

This question tests expertise with closures, per-id memoization, and real-time rendering patterns in frontend systems. It specifically targets race-condition handling and efficient UI updates under high-frequency data scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Closure-scoped per-id caches for memory efficiency
- Per-id memoization with bounded history management
- Promise chaining to serialize renders and handle race conditions
- Debounce using requestAnimationFrame for smooth UI updates

## Code Example

```javascript
function createDataUpdater() {
  const cache = new Map(); // id -> [last 5 results]
  let renderPromise = Promise.resolve();
  let renderScheduled = false;

  const updateCache = (id, data) => {
    const history = cache.get(id) || [];
    history.push(data);
    if (history.length > 5) history.shift();
    cache.set(id, history);
  };

  const render = () => {
    renderScheduled = false;
    // Apply cache data to UI
  };

  const scheduleRender = () => {
    if (!renderScheduled) {
      renderScheduled = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(render);
    }
  };

  return {
    processBatch: (batch) => {
      renderPromise = renderPromise.then(() => {
        batch.forEach(item => updateCache(item.id, item.data));
        scheduleRender();
      });
    }
  };
}
```

## Trade-offs

**Pros:**
- Closure ensures encapsulation and prevents external cache mutation
- Bounded cache prevents memory leaks from unbounded growth
- Promise chain guarantees render ordering consistency
- requestAnimationFrame provides optimal browser rendering timing

**Cons:**
- Added complexity compared to simple direct updates
- Memory overhead from maintaining per-id histories
- Potential render lag if batches arrive faster than processing capacity
- Cache management adds computational overhead per item

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of closures, Promise deduplication, request cancellation, and caching strategies in building a production-ready fetch helper. It also touches on memory management with LRU caches and handling abortable requests.

## Key Concepts
- Closures for per-key state isolation
- Promise deduplication to prevent redundant network requests
- Cancellation with AbortController for stale requests
- LRU cache implementation using Map for ordered storage
- Edge cases: rapid toggling between keys and stale data handling

## Code Example
```javascript
const api = (() => {
  const inFlight = new Map();
  const cache = new Map();
  const MAX_CACHE = 50;

  function memoFetch(key, options = {}) {
    if (cache.has(key)) {
      const entry = cache.get(key);
      cache.delete(key);
      cache.set(key, entry);
      return Promise.resolve(entry);
    }

    if (inFlight.has(key)) {
      return inFlight.get(key);
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();
    const promise = fetch(key, { ...options, signal: controller.signal })
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => {
        inFlight.delete(key);
        if (cache.size >= MAX_CACHE) {
          const firstKey = cache.keys().next().value;
          cache.delete(firstKey);
        }
        cache.set(key, data);
        return data;
      })
      .catch(err => {
        inFlight.delete(key);
        throw err;
      });

    inFlight.set(key, { promise, controller });
    return promise;
  }

  function cancel(key) {
    if (inFlight.has(key)) {
      inFlight.get(key).controller.abort();
      inFlight.delete(key);
    }
  }

  return { memoFetch, cancel };
})();
```

